---
import { mastheadStamp } from "../../dossiers/headline.js";
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';import EveryCut from '../../components/EveryCut.astro';import GuideData from '../../components/GuideData.astro';
import {bySlug,kicker} from '../../dossiers/registry.js';
import {timeUseModel} from '../../dossiers/time-use.model.js';
import {timeUseChart,timeUseNumbers} from '../../dossiers/time-use.charts.js';
const D=bySlug['time-use'],model=timeUseModel();
const N=timeUseNumbers(model);
const groups=[...new Set(model.plates.map(p=>p.group))];
const period=model.plates[0].period;
const inputs='all time-use bundles';
---
<Base title="Time use · Measure of India" description={D.description} nav="guides" dateStamp={mastheadStamp(D)} crumbLeft={`<a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / Households / <b>Time use</b>`} crumbRight={`${D.statusNote}, <b>${D.version}</b>`} canonical="/guides/time-use/" ogImage="/og/time-use.png">
<div class="page">
<div class="titleblock break" data-inputs="time-use-2024-t8-minutes" data-prose="evergreen and data-bound facts"><div class="kicker">{kicker(D)}</div><div class="hero-grid"><h1 set:html={D.headline}/><p class="lede">Someone cooks the meal, washes the clothes, feeds a child and takes an older relative to the doctor. Most of this work earns no wage, and most of it is done by women. India’s Time Use Survey records a whole day for every person it visits, so this work can be counted beside jobs, school, rest and sleep.</p></div><div class="meta"><span>Survey <b>{period}</b></span><span>People <b>Aged six and above</b></span><span>Source <b>National Statistics Office</b></span></div>
<div class="numbers"><div class="num"><div class="l">Domestic work: females</div><div class="v acc" id="n-domestic_female">{N["n-domestic_female"]}</div><div class="d">A day, averaged over everyone, including those who did none</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">Domestic work: males</div><div class="v" id="n-domestic_male">{N["n-domestic_male"]}</div><div class="d">On the same basis</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">Care: females</div><div class="v" id="n-care_female">{N["n-care_female"]}</div><div class="d">Looking after children and adults in the household</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">Care: males</div><div class="v" id="n-care_male">{N["n-care_male"]}</div><div class="d">On the same basis</div></div></div></div>
<section data-inputs="time-use-2024-t8-minutes" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Work does not end at the front door</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="wm-grid"><div class="wm-main"><p>A wage shows how a job is paid. It does not show how much work a person does in a day. Cooking for the family, minding a child and growing food for the household’s own plate are all work, and none of them is paid.</p><p>The survey asks a member of each household to walk through the previous day, from early morning to early the next morning, in half-hour slots. From that it measures who did each activity, for how long, and how the day divides between work, school, leisure and sleep.</p><p>It describes what people did. It cannot say how freely they chose it, how tiring it was, or what would change if their lives changed.</p></div><div class="wm-rail"><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">Whose average?</span>The figures include everyone aged six and above, so girls and boys are counted alongside women and men. Female and male follow the survey’s categories.</aside></div></div></section>
<section id="how-to-read" data-inputs={inputs} data-prose="evergreen methodology"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Three ways of asking about time</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p><b>Who did it.</b> The share of people who did the activity at all during the day. A person does many things in a day, so these shares do not add up to 100%.</p><p><b>For how long, among those who did it.</b> The average time among the people who did the activity. People who did none are left out, so these averages do not add up to a day.</p><p><b>For how long, across everyone.</b> The average across the whole group, counting those who did none as zero. It is the first measure times the second, and these averages do add up to 24 hours.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p><b>Two things at once.</b> Up to three activities can be recorded in a half-hour slot. Unless a chart says otherwise, the figures here count every recorded activity and split a shared slot equally between them, so a mother cooking while minding a child is counted for both.</p><p><b>Different kinds of work.</b> Paid work, production of goods for the household’s own use (farming, fetching water, collecting fuel), domestic work and care are separate categories. Employment in this survey includes travel to work and work in family enterprises, so it is broader than paid work alone.</p></div></div></section>
<section id="current-reading" data-inputs="time-use-2024-t6-pct, time-use-2024-t7-minutes, time-use-2024-t8-minutes, time-use-2024-t18-minutes, time-use-2024-t26-minutes, time-use-2024-t42-minutes" data-prose="deterministic source-traced facts"><div class="sec-head"><h2>What the survey found</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">{period}</span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>Across everyone aged six and above, females spent <b id="n-domestic_gap">{N["n-domestic_gap"]}</b> more a day on domestic work than males. Employment runs the other way: <b id="n-employment_male">{N["n-employment_male"]}</b> a day across males, <b id="n-employment_female">{N["n-employment_female"]}</b> across females. Both are averages over everyone, not the length of a working shift.</p><p>The domestic gap is in who does it and in how long. <b id="n-domestic_female_participation">{N["n-domestic_female_participation"]}</b> of females did some domestic work during the day, against <b id="n-domestic_male_participation">{N["n-domestic_male_participation"]}</b> of males. Those who did it spent <b id="n-domestic_female_participant">{N["n-domestic_female_participant"]}</b> and <b id="n-domestic_male_participant">{N["n-domestic_male_participant"]}</b> respectively.</p><p>A job does not make it go away. Among people with regular salaried jobs who did domestic work, females spent <b id="n-salaried_domestic_female">{N["n-salaried_domestic_female"]}</b> on it and males <b id="n-salaried_domestic_male">{N["n-salaried_domestic_male"]}</b>.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>Nor does money, at least in this survey. Females who did domestic work in the poorest fifth of households spent <b id="n-spending_lowest">{N["n-spending_lowest"]}</b> on it; in the richest fifth, <b id="n-spending_highest">{N["n-spending_highest"]}</b>.</p><p>Counting paid and unpaid work together, females aged six and above worked <b id="n-total_work_female">{N["n-total_work_female"]}</b> a day and males <b id="n-total_work_male">{N["n-total_work_male"]}</b>. Between fifteen and fifty-nine the figures are <b id="n-working_age_work_female">{N["n-working_age_work_female"]}</b> and <b id="n-working_age_work_male">{N["n-working_age_work_male"]}</b>.</p><p>Males also have more leisure: <b id="n-leisure_male">{N["n-leisure_male"]}</b> a day on culture, media, games and sport, against <b id="n-leisure_female">{N["n-leisure_female"]}</b> for females.</p></div></div></section>
{groups.map(group=><section data-inputs={[...new Set(model.plates.filter(p=>p.group===group).flatMap(p=>p.sourceBundles))].join(', ')} data-prose="evergreen explanations and data-bound facts"><div class="sec-head"><h2>{group}</h2><span class="line"></span></div>{model.plates.filter(p=>p.group===group).map(p=><div class="break"><figure id={'cb-'+p.id} data-inputs={p.sourceBundles.join(', ')}><div class="plate-head"><div class="t"><h3>{p.title}</h3><div class="sub">{p.sub}</div></div><div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id={'pv-'+p.id}>{N['pv-'+p.id]}</div><div class="c">{p.lead.label}</div></div></div><div class="frame"><div class="plotzone" id={'plot-'+p.id} set:html={timeUseChart(p)} /><div class="plotfoot"><span class="src">{p.source}, {p.period}.</span><span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href={p.sourceUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Original table ↗</a></span></div></div><div class="explain"><div class="ev"><span class="mk">How to read this</span><p>{p.explain}</p></div></div></figure></div>)}</section>)}
<section data-inputs={inputs} data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Common misreadings</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p><b>“Married women do more housework, so marriage causes it.”</b> Married and never-married people differ in age, work and household. The tables describe groups; they do not isolate the effect of marriage, a job or more money.</p><p><b>“An average describes a typical couple.”</b> Female and male averages come from different people. They do not describe how two people in the same home share the work.</p><p><b>“Add housework and childcare shares to get the share doing unpaid work.”</b> The same person often does both. The survey publishes its own combined measure, used in the paid-and-unpaid chart.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p><b>“A zero means nobody did it.”</b> Small averages round to zero. Where the survey had no sample at all, the table prints a dash; those cells are kept as gaps, never as zero.</p><p><b>“Recorded care minutes are the whole care burden.”</b> Being on call for a sick parent or a sleeping baby is hard to record in half-hour slots. The survey counts what was reported, not the responsibility behind it.</p><p><b>“A state near the top is different from its neighbour.”</b> Usually it is not. The range chart above shows how much sampling error each state estimate carries.</p></div></div></section>
<EveryCut slug="time-use"/><GuideData slug="time-use"/>
<section data-inputs={inputs} data-prose="provenance and scope"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Sources</h2><span class="line"></span></div><p>Every figure comes from the original detailed tables and sampling-error workbooks that the National Statistics Office links from its report <i>Time Use in India</i>, {period}: 62 workbooks, read cell by cell, each value keeping its workbook, sheet and cell address. The charts above answer one question each; every published table and cut is in the table above and the downloads. Where the report prints a dash for a cut with no sample, the cell is kept as a gap.</p><p>The ministry’s data API carries some of the same tables. A check against the report found one table whose values do not match its stated basis and some minute values labelled as percentages, so the report cells are used here. The 2019 survey used a similar design, but its tables have not yet been checked against this edition, so the two are not joined into a trend.</p><p><a href="https://mospi.gov.in/publication/time-use-india-2024">National Statistics Office: Time Use in India</a>. Each chart links its own original table.</p></section>
</div><script>import '../../dossiers/guide.client.js';</script>
</Base>
